<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-outcomes-1">

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-R1">
    <title>R1 From Derivative Graph to Function Graph</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-R1">
      <title>R1</title>
      <p>
        I can interpret the graph of a rate of change or the derivative <m>f'(x)</m>
        to describe and construct the graph of the function <m>f(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    
    <exercise label="R1_Graph_From_Derivative">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following graph is the graph of <m>y=f'(x)</m>, where <m>f'(x)</m> is the derivative of another function <m>f(x)</m>. Interpret <m>f'(x)</m> in order to draw a potential graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>. Your graph must clearly demonstrate correct intervals for increasing, decreasing, and concavity. Label the x-values for all local extremes and inflection points.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="R1_exercise_derivative_graph">
          <caption>Graph of <m>f'(x)</m></caption>
          <image width="75%">
            <shortdescription>Graph of a smooth function roughly like a rounded W that crosses the x-axis at -3, -1, 1, and 3. The graph is above the x-axis to the left of -3, between -1 and 1, and again to the right of 3; and it is below the x-axis between -3 and -1 and again between 1 and 3. The graph shows a valley at approximately x=-2.25 and another at x=+2.25, with a peak value at x=0.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}
                [ xmin=-5.5, xmax=5.5, ymin=-8.5, ymax=8.5, axis lines=center, grid=both, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y=f'(x)$}, 
                minor x tick num=1, minor y tick num=4 ]
                \addplot [domain=-5.5:5.5, samples=100, very thick] { (x^2-1)*(x^2-9)/4 };
                \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-R2">
    <title>R2 Definite Integrals for Net Change in Quantity</title>
    
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-R2">
      <title>R2</title>
      <p>
        I can calculate the definite integral of a rate function
        using geometric area formulas and interpret the value as
        the total increment of the changing value.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="R2_Integral_For_Net_Change">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The graph below represents the rate <m>r(t)</m> at which water is being added to a tank (or removed, as appropriate) in gallons/minute. 
          If the tank started (<m>t=0</m>) with 15 gallons, state and use an appropriate definite integral to find the amount of water at <m>t=10</m> minutes.
          A correct solution must show the correct notation for the definite integral and calculate its value using appropriate geometric formulas.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="R2_exercise_rate_graph">
          <caption>Graph of the rate of change of water in gal/min</caption>
          <image width="75%">
            <shortdescription>
              Graph of a rate function made from piecewise linear functions.
              It has a constant rate with a value of 2 gal/min between the times 0 and 2 minutes.
              It changes linearly between times 2 and 3 minutes to reach a rate of 0 gal/min at 3 minutes.
              It then changes linearly between times 3 and 5 minutes to reach a rate of -6 gal/min at 5 minutes, then changes back to 0 gal/min in a linear way at 7 minutes.
              It then increases linearly to 4 gal/min at 8 minutes.
              It remains constant at 4 gal/min until 10 minutes.
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}[
                  width=8cm, height=6cm,
                  axis lines=middle,
                    grid=major,
                    axis on top,
                  xlabel={$t$ (min)}, ylabel={$r(t)$ (gal/min)},
                  xlabel style={anchor=west}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
                  xmin=-0.5, xmax=10.5, ymin=-8, ymax=6,
                  xtick={2,3,5,7,8}, ytick={2,4,-6},
                  extra x ticks={0,10},
                  clip=false,
                ]
                  \draw[thick,blue]
                    (axis cs:0,2) -- (axis cs:2,2) -- (axis cs:3,0) --
                    (axis cs:5,-6) -- (axis cs:7,0) -- (axis cs:8,4) -- (axis cs:10,4);
                \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-R3">
    <title>R3 Applying Properties of Definite Integrals</title>
    
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-R3">
      <title>R3</title>
      <p>
        I can apply a combination of the properties of definite integrals,
        including the splitting property, the sum rule, and the constant multiple rule,
        to compute the value of a definite integral involving a combination of
        two functions.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="R3_Definite_Integral_Properties">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that for a function <m>f(x)</m> (not shown) we have
          <m>\displaystyle \int_1^3 f(x) \, dx = 5</m> and
          <m>\displaystyle \int_1^8 f(x) \,dx = 2</m>.
          A graph of <m>g(x)</m> is shown below with regions between the graph and the <m>x</m>-axis labeled with areas <m>R_1=4</m>, <m>R_2=3</m> and <m>R_3=6</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="R3_exercise_function_with_regions">
          <caption>Graph of g(x)</caption>
          <image width="75%">
            <shortdescription>
              Graph of a function that is above the axis when x is less than 3,
              below the axis for x values between 3 and 5, and then above the axis for x greater than 5. Three regions are created with areas between the x-axis and the function graph: 
              R1 is above the axis and created with x going from 1 to 3,
              R2 is below the axis and created with x going from 3 to 5,
              and R3 is above the axis and created with x going from 5 to 8.
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}[
                  width=11cm, height=6cm,
                  axis lines=middle,
                  axis on top,
                  xlabel={$x$},
                  xlabel style={anchor=west},
                  xmin=-1.7, xmax=9.8, ymin=-1.6, ymax=1.7,
                  xtick={1,3,5,8},
                  major tick style={thick, black},
                  major tick length=7pt,
                  ytick=\empty,
                  clip=false,
                ]
                  % shaded regions, drawn first so the curve sits on top
                  \fill[blue!15] (axis cs:1,0)
                    -- plot[smooth, tension=0.7] coordinates {
                      (axis cs:1,0.9) (axis cs:1.6,1.15) (axis cs:2.2,1.15) (axis cs:2.7,0.5) (axis cs:3,0)
                    }
                    -- cycle;
                  \fill[blue!15] (axis cs:3,0)
                    -- plot[smooth, tension=0.7] coordinates {
                      (axis cs:3,0) (axis cs:3.4,-0.6) (axis cs:4,-1.0) (axis cs:4.6,-0.6) (axis cs:5,0)
                    }
                    -- cycle;
                  \fill[blue!15] (axis cs:5,0)
                    -- plot[smooth, tension=0.7] coordinates {
                      (axis cs:5,0) (axis cs:5.6,0.7) (axis cs:6.5,1.1) (axis cs:7.3,1.0) (axis cs:8,0.8)
                    }
                    -- (axis cs:8,0)
                    -- cycle;

                  % the curve itself, drawn continuously (extends past x=1 and x=8)
                  \draw[thick, blue] plot[smooth, tension=0.7] coordinates {
                    (axis cs:-1,0.3) (axis cs:-0.3,0.55) (axis cs:0.4,0.5) (axis cs:1,0.9)
                    (axis cs:1.6,1.15) (axis cs:2.2,1.15) (axis cs:2.7,0.5) (axis cs:3,0)
                    (axis cs:3.4,-0.6) (axis cs:4,-1.0) (axis cs:4.6,-0.6) (axis cs:5,0)
                    (axis cs:5.6,0.7) (axis cs:6.5,1.1) (axis cs:7.3,1.0) (axis cs:8,0.8)
                    (axis cs:8.6,0.5) (axis cs:9,0.65) (axis cs:9.5,0.45)
                  };

                  % function label
                  \node[anchor=south west] at (axis cs:8.7,0.85) {$y=g(x)$};

                  % vertical connectors at x=1 and x=8
                  \draw[dashed] (axis cs:1,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.9);
                  \draw[dashed] (axis cs:8,0) -- (axis cs:8,0.8);

                  % region labels
                  \node at (axis cs:2,0.5) {$R_1$};
                  \node at (axis cs:4,-0.4) {$R_2$};
                  \node at (axis cs:6.4,0.45) {$R_3$};
                \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Use this information to find <m>\displaystyle \int_3^8 2f(x) + g(x) \, dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
  
</section>
